namesakes: this page is about FORENAMES, not surnames. It is titled "The forenames" and its subject is a given name travelling through daughters as reliably as a surname travels through sons. Defranceski's namesakes page collects notable strangers of the same surname; Falco's argues for not having one at all.
sources: STILL EXEMPT, AND THE REASON HAS CHANGED — 9 October 2026. The old reason was that sources.json was four tables of holdings and the bibliography was still prose, so the restructuring had to happen before harmonisation could be considered. THAT RESTRUCTURING IS DONE: every row now carries an id derived from its own first cell, a title, a kind, and a `held` saying where the paper is, and seventeen pages declare against those ids. So the old blocker is gone, the page is still exempt, and the reason is now the real one. The kit's Sources row is title / where / url. Two of these four tables carry PERSON and DIED columns, and a person is not a source — the estates table exists to say whose file MHG 5155/51 is and when he died, which is what makes an unread reference worth ordering. Drawing it with the shared row would flatten those columns away and leave a list of reference numbers. AN EXEMPTION WHOSE STATED REASON HAS BEEN OVERTAKEN IS WORSE THAN NO EXEMPTION, because it reads as considered and is not. This file prints every line on every run so that somebody eventually notices, and that is what happened.
dna: two kits and no ethnicity estimate at all. The subject here is a COUSINHOOD — who the matches are, which side they sit on, and an aunt whose chart is the purest in the family because her father's side is Argentine and English. Nothing on this page is the same object as Falco's two-person admixture comparison or Luwinski's haplogroup.
